{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{(s+5)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fbbc616-425b-534a-bc12-f283a3ccb1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s-5)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441244} :answer-field/id #uuid "dceb88f7-92bd-5036-a0c6-c37c572187a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b781ac41-d289-50fe-8eb5-284b3b53ff9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331765" :question/problem "Consider a function $h(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{h(t)\\}=H(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{5t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $H(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t=H(s-5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answe