answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(s-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b0c454-6ede-5a67-b7e0-9e57a958790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(s+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441270} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc08b231-e40a-5e8d-8f0a-dd2d12c01d08" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "593b9890-14ef-5ad1-b4d2-54793d0a7bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331804" :question/problem "Consider a function $n(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{n(t)\\}=N(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $N(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t=N(s+4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177fa7ee-9b47-5b28-b20c-baed96a9d314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(s+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441221} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc1d439-9963-58cb-a205-d61ca3a432cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s+3)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441238} {:answer/id #uuid "698ab393-4cb8-5c51-801d-01a9a0a55b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s+4)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441253} {:answer/id #uuid "92467dfc-7fb3-5ffa-ba45-6e28719e9f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s-3)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441265} {:answer/id #uuid "ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441272} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd7b592-4a98-55bf-97ff-8128ee4c0d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b82c8fbc-5979-517c-956f-f5afc90605c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331827" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{4}\\}=\\frac{4!}{{s}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\} & =\\frac{4!}{(s+3)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bc22d0e-6715-5dd0-ab8e-b518e61d3e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-4}{(s-4)^{2}+49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441224} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc22650-741c-5792-87dd-c71550abbc25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441231} {:answer/id #uuid "3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441239} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4907c7-8c42-50dc-b370-5907d5a52206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s-4)^{2}+49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441242} {:answer/id #uuid "dde48221-516d-591b-9446-b360359d4e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+4)^{2}+49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441239} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a144e16-0a72-5286-bcc9-e70abbff2563" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ea945f3-bef2-521b-aa15-dff430408da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-331855" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{7}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\} & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea27038-03d7-5405-b680-15df02fceab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303033} {:answer/id #uuid "7d261f62-a215-5cab-9a32-3f9873b831ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303237} {:answer/id #uuid "a689eeff-fafc-5013-b3f1-24e7bf3ac6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303478} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb7c1d2-5303-5915-b07b-79a0ecfeec04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303718} {:answer/id #uuid "db6afb8e-6f9c-51db-b412-cba19033f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb7c1d2-5303-5915-b07b-79a0ecfeec04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303718} :answer-field/id #uuid "3956f331-82d9-5a12-8507-4fc3e5638275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "050795d3-56ec-5162-9d13-5f770f4a560b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331860" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1003b9-4336-57aa-86fd-d5e21ab2eb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{(s-4)^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302931} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe90190-2cf7-5464-8d8a-a650e6f8ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302957} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef3c2bd-911c-5207-813a-6b7556254441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-4}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303445} {:answer/id #uuid "aa79092f-9062-510c-99ee-d9aba5103524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s+4)^{2}-9}{((s+4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303507} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e6c536-ba00-52cd-a829-d7109b17cfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e6c536-ba00-52cd-a829-d7109b17cfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303963} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e76e4b-092d-5811-b802-05998a840f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e98f8075-941a-5e1c-9a37-f46cd62e24a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331872" :question/problem "Given that  $L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a2b3a2-2a1c-5657-9a0c-46c3d1d0b071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $2$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220291} {:answer/id #uuid "c68eb1a4-0e89-5167-8c50-da7595818ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220292} {:answer/id #uuid "45a5925f-203c-5ff0-ba1d-13bd03e18a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $2$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220293} {:answer/id #uuid "52465464-3cc9-5d3e-be71-9ddda92ffee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220294} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d9e20-287a-52d4-ae92-343b1c4d6e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52465464-3cc9-5d3e-be71-9ddda92ffee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220294} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c83dc8-2008-503f-a501-9ad228213d1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fa333ab-b95e-5e37-a5a1-bded577fcc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-332055" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054b45a4-571c-51d5-8916-deb99bc5b866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302492} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca22da3-5ee1-5636-8493-a9e95b0feb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302938} {:answer/id #uuid "99cc8503-b394-5afa-bcf3-b92224bdd7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303409} {:answer/id #uuid "d181ebc5-ac4e-53ee-a2b6-83f29a3544fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303748} {:answer/id #uuid "d55441dc-9527-50c3-8c30-0068b0b8c212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d181ebc5-ac4e-53ee-a2b6-83f29a3544fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303748} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b950977-e300-5887-b9b0-d9617038a686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a494f0da-488f-5d2c-a912-87d73ea366c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332059" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebfe2578-7c5f-5df7-9d72-39a56be2386e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8d/8daba7e586feeff02624e5d0fb508c9d2adedb23d5a79fe329f83a42e1f8256e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368083} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c1dfa-8219-593c-b78b-6240e600685c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368084} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c5a3d-eb7d-5081-ba8b-a26f602f1890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6d497165f1dbc9fd9db77e6a8943f6acd45262cb74ebafd4a418d612be884b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368085} {:answer/id #uuid "134ca311-f8fb-5d46-8687-e4802cae00a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368086} {:answer/id #uuid "63430f1f-817b-5c2a-bd77-427ce4f138a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ef/ef457f3f2030bd9399e707ea5bc32f9fe58aae29f848c270c5b971afd98315bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "134ca311-f8fb-5d46-8687-e4802cae00a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368086} :answer-field/id #uuid "84509ec0-1233-5211-bf4c-8b7806b1f61b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2acfbcc3-d517-5f68-a1bd-d612fba90c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-332104" :question/problem "![](images/82/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01504f10-66fd-598a-98b7-004911e742cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s+1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441213} {:answer/id #uuid "226789de-c6c7-5856-86e1-65ea12a70479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441226} {:answer/id #uuid "285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441230} {:answer/id #uuid "741a34dc-db4b-5f21-90b7-c70416fe805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441256} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f0bbc4-71bc-5a80-9a09-af07edb9ffe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{4}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441230} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f73d16d-36f0-5d60-9f4e-d0a31d53ec05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a4eb980-3664-5af2-945a-e7530601aa00" :question/math-academy-id "q-332174" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}$  for $s>1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{3{e}^{t}\\}+L\\{4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$then$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}+4\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a660670-5b82-51e4-a4ab-75bcae056619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302526} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4b8dff-d7ea-505c-a49d-8634294ac8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302825} {:answer/id #uuid "c97503fc-5bd7-579d-87d8-6fa4397ab911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s-1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303692} {:answer/id #uuid "e47fa138-b693-5da5-97e9-fe4656a6782a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303878} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b266e2-584a-5034-8951-74c07eb426bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a660670-5b82-51e4-a4ab-75bcae056619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d437068-b018-5153-b0ab-ced1037b5d47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75147345-f194-5790-8fe4-ff587f8030d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332183" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878ecff6-a045-5595-bd46-b23e255ce047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368097} {:answer/id #uuid "3368f758-7753-54d9-9a94-24de4fff6921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{10}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368098} {:answer/id #uuid "2b16dd07-1cb7-536e-a033-0738cd8c6049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368099} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7bea03-166d-517e-82ce-2d9193e98053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368100} {:answer/id #uuid "3f82a2b5-0ff1-5e48-a8d3-81fc3ec2e66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{5}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb7bea03-166d-517e-82ce-2d9193e98053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368100} :answer-field/id #uuid "57a3b290-92fa-54bf-93a0-2c8ff4a08c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20b395e8-6914-5e1c-ade5-fc76b7cc76a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332198" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)={e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186d0804-d9b7-5c1e-a48b-f2497e2f89ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f20e213-0393-5db6-aa8d-bdc2e21fb5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s-4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302852} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e6ad2-5cf7-566d-b99b-539b2a803a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebb0b7d-433c-517d-8ef8-4167f92e9fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s+2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303133} {:answer/id #uuid "e1abe34e-4cb8-53ca-9efd-417f01fc56ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e6ad2-5cf7-566d-b99b-539b2a803a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6eec93c-0666-55e7-b6ac-2e919c02dc8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d34dedd9-0917-526a-8fa5-cdd60c269ceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332199" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}\\}+L\\{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05595157-cac2-5680-a2b2-64951fa393e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{60}{(s+7)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441214} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc3f69e-98b2-53fa-8063-59aefa158407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s-6)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441232} {:answer/id #uuid "3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441237} {:answer/id #uuid "64a8af15-f8b3-5848-a8ef-a51af4a1f066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{60}{(s-7)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441250} {:answer/id #uuid "c13be24b-9c27-5e28-a002-16e6cff5ffdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s+7)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90c7511-6cb7-52d0-a241-3716bc5adacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02d9f6f3-7af5-52c4-b620-b99c6f0b589d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341501" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{5}\\}=\\frac{5!}{{s}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\} & =\\frac{5!}{(s-7)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7688a707-770a-5d26-8326-a3a0e9adea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391764} {:answer/id #uuid "53a678d2-9902-5f47-87db-4b0cf14c0bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd173c18910a9b2c428e7c4e5c52e13935a2439442610fea134ac9bb63fdf01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391765} {:answer/id #uuid "196682d7-8bb1-57df-ac61-d81412a74a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afbe8291979d90666756181ca898851c5f0f30755ba985c32d56bd4b65e1fa5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391766} {:answer/id #uuid "b7743ef4-153d-5bf1-9a95-2e67eec1dc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15227beae514ccbfe85f3ba5d1ac4bd47fc130c5c15ff4ecd4de1a2813f8f2db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391767} {:answer/id #uuid "93d16724-e79f-567d-8131-03ecce9ca2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7688a707-770a-5d26-8326-a3a0e9adea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391764} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3194f8-8dc6-5b04-9805-27bc947704b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e769abe-27f5-51cc-8d1a-a0d37ed70a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341512" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-6t}f(t)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-6t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441216} {:answer/id #uuid "111d64d2-13e2-51be-bf85-4c204ca01df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441219} {:answer/id #uuid "36bc25f3-49a1-5de6-b0c1-7362aa7b3d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s+1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441236} {:answer/id #uuid "58987a2a-0210-5329-9024-4178a6b1aae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}+2}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441247} {:answer/id #uuid "87ad7f9c-8811-5c95-b910-5eb165b987c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-1}{s(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441216} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a092a7-1df9-5b92-bae4-dac2a2473380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b26511a8-09e9-54a7-8467-ef5beee0c7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-64307" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=t({e}^{2t}-1)$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t({e}^{2t}-1)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}-t\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}=\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}]}
